      "length_km": 72,
      "overall_status": "severely_degraded",
      "cpcb_class": "E (effectively below E)",
      "description": "A 796 km navigational canal running parallel to the Coromandel Coast; the Chennai stretch (~72 km) serves as the city's primary stormwater and sewage drainage artery, connecting to the Cooum and Adyar rivers.",
      "notes": "Originally built by the British for inland navigation, the Buckingham Canal now receives untreated sewage from 400+ illegal outlets along the Chennai stretch. It effectively functions as an open drain rather than a navigable waterway. The canal's inability to discharge freely into the sea during monsoon creates severe backwater flooding in low-lying areas of North and South Chennai. TNPCB monitoring consistently records near-zero dissolved oxygen throughout the Chennai stretch.",
